<section id="s-intro" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Curvature Dilemma</div>
<h2>Why Gradient Descent Gets Stuck in the Valley</h2>
<p>In high-dimensional loss landscapes, optimization is rarely bottlenecked by the gradient's size, but by how rapidly the landscape curves. In neural networks, particularly Transformers, this landscape is highly heterogeneous—exhibiting a small number of extremely steep (high-curvature) directions and many flat (low-curvature) directions.</p>
<p>When you optimize with standard Gradient Descent (GD) or coordinate-wise adaptive methods like Adam, the update is proportional to the gradient. Consequently, the update gets pulled heavily towards the steep, high-gradient walls. The result? The optimizer repeatedly overshoots the valley floor, causing wild oscillations that force the use of small learning rates.</p>
<p>Muon, however, applies a <strong>spectral normalization</strong> directly to the gradient matrix. By setting all singular values of the matrix update to the same scale, Muon distributes its update energy evenly between flat and steep directions. This prevents overshooting and allows it to travel directly down the valley floor.</p>

<div class="vp">
