<section id="s-math" data-code="math">
<div class="section-no">02 — One-Step Loss Decomposition</div>
<h2>Where the Muon Advantage Appears</h2>
<p>To pinpoint the source of Muon's advantage, the authors analyze the loss progress made during a single training step. Using a second-order Taylor expansion on the loss landscape, we can decompose the realized one-step loss decrease into first-order gain and second-order curvature penalty:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Taylor Expansion Decomposition</div>
  <span class="math-var">ΔL(W, Z)</span> = L(W) - L(W - Z) ≈ <span class="math-var">⟨G, Z⟩</span> - <span class="math-var">0.5 * ⟨Z, H[Z]⟩</span>
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment"># where G is the gradient, Z is the parameter update, and H is the Hessian operator</span>
</div>

<p>Comparing Adam and Muon at matched validation loss during pretraining on FineWeb, a surprising result emerges: **their first-order gains are almost identical**. The entire optimization gap is explained by the second-order term. Adam consistently incurs a much larger curvature penalty, limiting its step-by-step progress, while Muon maintains a tiny second-order penalty.</p>

<div class="vp">
