<section id="s-sharpness" data-code="sharpness">
<div class="section-no">03 — NDS vs Update Norm</div>
<h2>Is it Step Size or Step Direction?</h2>
<p>Does Muon pay a smaller curvature penalty simply because it takes smaller steps? To separate the scale of the update from its direction, we factorize the curvature penalty by defining **Normalized Directional Sharpness (NDS)**:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Normalized Directional Sharpness (NDS)</div>
  <span class="math-var">S_F(W; Z)</span> = ⟨Z, H[Z]⟩ / ||Z||_F^2
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment"># Curvature Penalty: I^(2) = 0.5 * ||Z||_F^2 * S_F(W; Z)</span>
</div>

<p>By measuring both update norms and NDS across training, we find that the update norm is nearly identical between the two optimizers. The entire difference in curvature penalty is driven by NDS. Muon's spectrally normalized update directions consistently find flatter paths through the parameter space, maintaining an average NDS ratio of 1.76 over Adam.</p>

<div class="vp">
