</section>

<section id="s-layers" data-code="layers">
<div class="section-no">04 — Layer-wise Curvature Decomposition</div>
<h2>How Layers Interact During Training</h2>
<p>Where does this NDS advantage live? We can decompose the model's total Hessian operator into diagonal (within-layer) and off-diagonal (cross-layer) blocks:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Within- vs Cross-layer Decomposition</div>
  <span class="math-var">S_F(W; Z)</span> = <span class="math-var">S_F_within(W; Z)</span> + <span class="math-var">S_F_cross(W; Z)</span>
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment"># within-layer captures single layer updates: H_ii</span>
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment"># cross-layer captures multi-layer interactions: H_ij (i ≠ j)</span>
</div>

<p>Early in training, cross-layer interactions dominate the curvature penalty for both optimizers. However, as training progresses, Muon's cross-layer interactions decay rapidly, and its within-layer share climbs from 14% to 44%. For Adam, within-layer share remains stable at around 30%. This shows that in the later stages, Muon's advantage is sustained by lower within-layer curvature, specifically concentrated in the shallowest and deepest layers.</p>

